@page "/utility-layout"

<CatHead Title="Layout utilities">
    The flex arrangements that would otherwise be an inline <code>style="display:flex; gap:8px"</code>
    — which is most of them. Everything on this page puts children in a line or a stack and
    decides how they share the space.
</CatHead>

<div class="cat-note">
    <i class="ri-stack-line"></i>
    <strong>The cascade layer is what makes these work.</strong> A utility is <code>(0,1,0)</code>
    and a component rule like <code>.table th, .table td</code> is <code>(0,1,1)</code>, so a utility
    loses on specificity alone. <code>sedna.utilities</code> sits after <code>sedna.paint</code>, so
    <code>.text-end</code> on a table cell wins regardless of it.
</div>

<div class="cat-note">
    <i class="ri-forbid-line"></i>
    <strong>Not a grid system.</strong> There are no breakpoint prefixes and no column classes,
    because the library has no breakpoint vocabulary and should not gain one. For a real page layout
    reach for <a href="/grid">Grids</a>, whose three primitives are breakpoint-free by construction.
</div>

<CatExample TExample="Examples.UtilityLayout.Rows" Title="Rows and columns" Demo="ex-demo--block">
    <p>
        <code>.sedna-row</code>, <code>.sedna-row-wrap</code> and <code>.sedna-col</code> are the three
        arrangements, each with the same 8px gap. <code>.sedna-push</code> is an empty span that eats
        the remaining space, so everything after it sits at the far end.
    </p>
    <p>
        These carry the <code>sedna-</code> prefix because they are library-owned utilities in a shared
        namespace, where <code>.row</code> and <code>.col</code> are names an app is very likely to
        have already.
    </p>
</CatExample>

<CatExample TExample="Examples.UtilityLayout.Alignment" Title="Alignment" Demo="ex-demo--block">
    <p>
        <code>.sedna-start</code>, <code>.sedna-center</code>, <code>.sedna-end</code> and
        <code>.sedna-baseline</code> set the cross axis; <code>.sedna-between</code> sets the main one.
        <code>.sedna-wrap</code> lets a <code>.sedna-row</code> break without turning it into a
        <code>.sedna-row-wrap</code>.
    </p>
    <p>
        <code>.sedna-baseline</code> is the one worth knowing: it is what lines two different type
        sizes up on the line they are written on, rather than on the middle of their boxes.
    </p>
</CatExample>

<CatExample TExample="Examples.UtilityLayout.Sizing" Title="Sizing" Demo="ex-demo--block">
    <p>
        <code>.sedna-fill</code> takes the remaining space <em>and</em> sets <code>min-width: 0</code>,
        which is the half people forget: a flex item refuses to shrink below its content by default, so
        <code>.text-truncate</code> inside one never shows its ellipsis without it.
    </p>
    <p>
        <code>.sedna-shrink-0</code> is its opposite, for the avatar and the badge that must keep
        their size. <code>.sedna-w-full</code> is a full-width block, usually a button in a narrow
        panel.
    </p>
</CatExample>

<CatExample TExample="Examples.UtilityLayout.Dividers" Title="Dividers" Demo="ex-demo--block">
    <p>
        <code>.sedna-divider-top</code> and <code>.sedna-divider-bottom</code> are a hairline between
        rows without an <code>&lt;hr&gt;</code>. Use them on a stack the library has no component for;
        a real list of records is a <a href="/collections"><code>.list</code></a>, which draws its own.
    </p>
</CatExample>
